    "body": "A song can cross borders even when listeners do not understand every word. Shakira became internationally known for music that blends pop with sounds influenced by her Colombian background and other musical traditions. She was born in Barranquilla, a city on Colombia's Caribbean coast. As a child, she loved dancing and experimenting with words. Her success grew from developing those interests into skills she could use in performances for audiences around the world.\n\n## Songs from a young age\n\nShakira began writing songs as a child. Songwriting combines several kinds of creative thinking. A writer chooses images and words, then fits them into the rhythm of the music. A singer learns how to communicate those ideas with pitch, breath, and expression. Not every early song becomes popular, and Shakira's first recordings did not make her famous everywhere. She continued practicing and eventually reached larger audiences through Spanish-language albums during the 1990s.\n\n## Singing in more than one language\n\nShakira later released songs in English as well as Spanish. Learning to communicate musically in different languages can be challenging. Words have different stresses and sounds, which affect where they fit into a melody. A songwriter may need to change more than vocabulary to make a song feel natural. Shakira's international music shows how artists can keep connections to a first language while experimenting with another.\n\n## Movement tells part of the story\n\nDance is an important part of many of Shakira's performances. She draws from several movement traditions, including forms of belly dance that she studied while growing up. In a concert, movement can emphasize the rhythm or help communicate a song's mood. A performer must coordinate dancing with singing, sometimes under bright lights and in front of enormous crowds. That requires rehearsal and physical stamina as well as musical skill.\n\n## Music and the World Cup\n\nShakira's song Waka Waka (This Time for Africa) became closely connected to the 2010 FIFA World Cup in South Africa. The recording brought global attention, though the music and dance draw on earlier African musical traditions. Understanding that context matters because famous performers often share sounds and ideas that began in other communities. Respectful music history recognizes the sources of inspiration rather than treating every popular version as an entirely new invention.\n\n## Listen like a music researcher\n\nChoose a school-appropriate song by an artist who performs in two languages. Notice how the rhythm, melody, and words interact. If you cannot understand the lyrics, consult a reliable translation and compare it with what the music made you feel. Shakira's career offers a chance to explore how artistic expression travels between languages and cultures. Listening carefully can reveal both the qualities that connect audiences and the traditions that make each performance distinctive.",
