    "body": "The men's 100-meter sprint can finish before someone has read this paragraph. Usain Bolt became one of the most famous athletes in the world by running that distance faster than anyone had officially recorded. At the 2009 World Championships in Berlin, he completed the 100 meters in 9.58 seconds, a world record that became a landmark in track and field. The performance looked almost effortless from the stands, but it depended on timing, technique, and training.\n\n## From Jamaica to the world\n\nUsain grew up in Jamaica, a Caribbean island with a strong track-and-field tradition. He began racing when he was young and eventually worked with coaches who helped develop his sprinting skills. Jamaica has produced many outstanding runners, and competitions at schools and clubs can introduce young athletes to the sport. Training includes much more than running full speed every day. Athletes practice starts, strength, movement patterns, and recovery between sessions.\n\n## What happens in a sprint?\n\nAt the starting signal, a runner pushes powerfully from starting blocks. The first steps are short and forceful as the athlete accelerates. Later, the stride becomes longer and the runner approaches top speed. The athlete must maintain good form while avoiding unnecessary movement. Usain was unusually tall for a sprinter, and his long strides became a recognizable feature. Height alone did not explain his success; coordination and speed were essential.\n\n## Records measured in hundredths\n\nA sprint result is often reported to hundredths of a second. If one runner finishes in 9.58 seconds and another in 9.68 seconds, the difference is only one tenth of a second. At elite speeds, that can separate a record from an ordinary result. Electronic timing systems and carefully defined rules make official comparisons possible. Fans may remember a dramatic pose after a finish, but the result itself depends on measurement.\n\n## More than one event\n\nUsain also became famous in the 200-meter sprint and the 4×100-meter relay. In a relay, teammates pass a baton and must complete exchanges within marked zones. Even a very fast group can lose if the baton is dropped or passed outside the allowed area. This makes the relay an example of teamwork inside a sport many people think of as individual competition. Practicing transitions is just as important as building running speed.\n\n## Look at the whole race\n\nWhen watching a sprint, compare the start, the middle, and the finish. The runner who leads after ten meters does not always win. Some athletes accelerate quickly, while others gain ground later. Study the movement instead of focusing only on the final time. Usain Bolt's 9.58-second record provides a memorable number, but understanding the preparation behind it offers a deeper lesson: extraordinary results often come from repeating ordinary skills with exceptional care.",
